ernment intervening  we're just doing him some more and so  this large stimulus package 787 billion  dollars doesn't do much to stimulate the  economy we tried that last year had a  big stimulus package in 2008 didn't work  doing it again you have a plan to deal  with the banks not articulated very  clearly the uncertainty again tanks the  market so it's it's really a  continuation in some sense even worse in  it I mean there is there an  inevitability about the rebounding of  the American economy and if that does  take place let's say in the next couple  of years does is it is it are we in a  difficult position as relatively  free-market people that the Obama  administration and its supporters will  take credit for for the recovery of the  American economy it's a possibility and  it's always a possibility to say this  worked and that didn't work and not  looking at the data hope people look at  the numbers because it really you can  really find out what's going on just so  just listen to somebody say something  look what actually happens it's pretty  convincing to me we have five chapters  here and and every one of them is pretty  much a question what caused the  financial crisis is chapter one 15 pages  can you give it to us in a couple of  sentences monetary excess is the Federal  Reserve took the interest rate down to  very low levels 1% kept it there for a  long time that generated the housing  boom because interest rates were so low  housing boom inevitably brings a bust  and all the foreclosures and all of the  bad debt that caused it comes from that  so monetary excesses second chapter is  another 10 to 15 pages on what prolonged  the crisis what prolonging the simplest  sort of thing is misdiagnosed it's like  a patient comes in got cancer doctor  says you got a digestive problem treats  it with something to give the  indigestion and the cancer gets worse  and in this case the misdiagnosis was  that they thought it was just a  liquidity problem not enough money in  fact it was a problem in the banks banks  had problems on their balance sheets  when you skip ahead to the fourth  chapter which is  a bit of a positive note what went right  and the two decades before the crisis I  can't imagine anything going right in  the economy at all but two decades  before things are going swimmingly  absolutely you know you think about it  was the the economists call this period  the Great Moderation or the long boom  because long expansions in really short  recessions people forget what big  recessions were but since the early 80s  all the way through actually now this is  a serious recession very smooth times  and again a lot of not a lot of  intervention kind of a hands-off policy  in many respects monetary policy filing  pretty clear guidelines a lot of  deregulation policies that freed up  markets lower tax rates all those things  which generated a consistently strong  economy for almost almost 25 years at  least two decades so now we are in the  difficulty we're in do you see a danger  that that we are going to have you know  obviously the amount of government  intervention we've had in the past past  a couple of months is frightening  re-regulation bad policy what do you see  as the idiotic solutions people very  concern with that because because again  now you know my story here isn't told  very much yeah story that government was  really at the center of this and causing  the problems that usually there's a  problem with the market so we've got  even do more yeah of these things you  know President Obama talking about the  only solution is government and so I'm  worried quite frankly that we're going  to be doing the wrong thing for a while  and it's gonna take a while to get back  on track you know it took when when  Reagan came in in the early 80s that was  it getting back on track issue because  we didn't have good performance in the  much of the 70s high inflation a lot of  instability so it's it's going to take  some e